014 in Berlin in the BCC conference. And this was also the host of EuroPython 2014. It's a conference that has a special place in my heart, personally speaking. specifically for this particular talk I had. It was called Traversing Mazes the Pythonic Way and Other Algorithmic Adventures. That was the title of the talk I proposed. So we're talking 2014, many years ago. Actually, I have a few excerpts from that talk. The videos are still online. So, yeah, internet is unforgiving. Audio doesn't work, apparently. Can we try that again? Oops. Okay. Thanks a lot for coming. I'm very excited to be here today. Okay. So look at that face. I was like, I'm very excited to be here today. I was dying, right? I was really excited to the point that I was really happy to be in that place of speaking to EuroPython Berlin for the first time. I wasn't really feeling excited because this was the room. And I was there. So I was dying inside. Because it was very dark. And for some reason they decided my talk was deserving the keynote room. It was a huge room. Anyway, it was definitely an experience. And actually the talk was talking about, was doing connections of Graf Traversals and Dungeons and Dragons, and apparently this has become like a recurring theme for me. If you like move forward 10 years later, EuroPython 2013, I did like, 2023, sorry, I did Each Hiker's Guide to Dungeons and Dragons. This is definitely a better talk. It's actually quite a nice talk. Very happy about that. So check that out if you're interested. But moving forward to the end of that talk. Let's see. And that's it. Thank you very much for your kind attention. Okay. Thank you very much, Valero. Any questions? You may recognize this question, lunch break. So please feel free to ask questions. Okay. So I think it was a very compact introduction. Thank you very much. I could say... That's it. So a few notes. I'm wearing the same T-shirt. Not incidentally. You might have recognised that person. That was Alexander, t