he time. And then the language evolved into, you know, from a scripting language to a powerhouse, So we had Python 2 at the time. I've been saying readable, teachable, powerful, elegant. This was just Python 2. Then we had transition from Python 2 to Python 3. There's been lots of debates about whether it was the right choice. We should ever migrate at all over the years. And then now nobody knows anything about that. Any newcomers doesn't use Python 2 at all. We start with Python 3. And the language has evolved in many ways over the years. We have F-strings, type-ins, async-await, pattern-matching, a beautiful, expressive, modern language. And Python 3.14 and beyond, we have free-threaded Python, no-guile, JIT compiler, deferred evaluation of annotation. The reason why I'm mentioning this is the language kept evolving over the years, and this is mostly because the community pushed to it. So, what is it that makes this community so special? First off, it's inclusive. It's safe, it's respectful, and it's open. It's a community where contributions are very welcome. New people contributing in a meaningful way. Everybody in this community is contributing in a meaningful way. And diverse and globally distributed. Over the years, it's not just about Python conferences but meetups by ladies, Jaguars, and many, many things. More importantly, it's organised by us. But where the community actually sit today, so ten years after Naomi's keynote, what has changed, really? Well, a lot has changed. The Python community in 2026 is bigger, more diverse and more global. And because of this, we're facing new challenges. We have new ways to gather. For example, during the pandemic, something was not right about conferences because we couldn't have conferences at the time, and so it was the start of online conferences. We had events like Pyjamas, PyData Global, events happening solely online with worldwide coverage. We had hybrid formats now, meaning conferences happening in real life of also attendees from remote attendees. So that's in-person or remote attendance. So you can reach out to people who can't travel for whatever reason. And as I said, it's not just big cities anymore. We have local Python groups, PyCon in Africa, PyCon APAC, Colombia, Pakistan, also local meetup. And I highlighted there university Python groups because I wanted to highlight that. The change is actually happening from the bottom of the community, from students, because nowadays, Python is taught at universities. It wasn't the case ten years ago. I also want to make a shout out to this project, which is Python deadline.es. I think it's Python deadline.es. That's a typo there. Otherwise, I ruined the joke. Sorry, Jesper. But it's a place where all the deadlines of Python conferences are actually collected. So if you're really looking for a venue to go to for a Python event, that's the tool you want to check out. And the reason why I think this is a perfect example of what I talk about is because community tools are built by community members. It's all sustained by the community itself in a very healthy way. And so how we communicate has changed as well. So in 2016, we'll be talking mailing lists, blogs, in-person events. In 2026, we talk about Discord, Mastodon, YouTube, Twitch, short-form videos, live coding, podcasts. Many, many things have changed. And there's also like the elephant in the room, like the AI question. AI changed who writes Python and how Python is written. This means like new voices, new rules, new users, new questions, and people who never thought they would code now they can code and so the question for us is how do we welcome them in our community and how we maintain depth with this new event and the takeaway here really is that sustainability matters open source maintainers burnout is real conference organizer fatigue is real and this is perhaps the most important question i dare to say for the next 10 years because everything isn't sustained and 